If a patient has a long-term-opioid-use and needs an additional-medication for an acute-pain and a nonopioid-medication is possible then every clinician should use the nonopioid-medication.
If a patient has a long-term-opioid-use and needs an additional-opioid for an acute-pain then every clinician should limit the additional-opioid for an expected-severe-pain-duration and should restore a baseline-opioid-dosage.
If a patient has a long-term-opioid-use and has a prolonged-around-the-clock-additional-opioid-use for a few-day-period then every clinician should use a baseline-dosage-taper.
If a patient has a long-term-opioid-use and receives an additional-opioid and the patient does not need the additional-opioid for a severe-acute-pain and there is a clinician then it is recommended that the clinician does not continue the additional-opioid.
